From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wtsapi32: a real console/services session, process lists, user config

- wtsapi32 treated every session, server and process call as remote
  Terminal Services infrastructure we don't have, so almost all of it
  either FIXME-stubbed or quietly lied (WTSEnumerateProcessesA always
  "succeeded" with an empty list). A desktop with no RDP role still has
  the two sessions a real Windows machine reports (session 0 "Services",
  disconnected; the console, active) and real local processes, so these
  now answer honestly instead of failing or faking success:
  - WTSOpenServer(Ex)A/W, WTSCloseServer: a NULL/empty/our-own name opens
    the local server; any other name fails RPC_S_SERVER_UNAVAILABLE (no
    RPC to a remote wtssvc). WTSOpenServerEx never fails, as documented.
  - WTSEnumerateServersA/W: this computer, the only server there is.
  - WTSEnumerateSessions(Ex)A/W: the Services and Console sessions.
  - WTSQuerySessionInformationA/W: WTSSessionInfo filled in completely
    (LogonTime/LastInputTime/ConnectTime were always zero before), plus
    WTSSessionId, WTSWinStationName, WTSClientBuildNumber/Name/Directory/
    ProductId/HardwareId/Address/Display, WTSIncoming/OutgoingBytes/Frames,
    WTSIsRemoteSession, WTSIdleTime, WTSLogonTime -- all either a real
    local value or the documented "no remote client" answer. The classes
    that need a Terminal Services role we don't have (WTSClientInfo,
    WTSSessionInfoEx, WTSConfigInfo, WTSValidationInfo, WTSSessionAddressV4)
    now fail ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED instead of a generic FIXME. The ANSI
    wrapper no longer runs WideCharToMultiByte over binary classes
    (WTSSessionId, WTSClientAddress, ...), which could read past the end
    of a non-string buffer, and now copies WTSSessionInfo's time/byte
    fields it used to silently drop.
  - WTSEnumerateProcessesA/WTSEnumerateProcessesExA: real conversions of
    WTSEnumerateProcessesExW's list (process names to ANSI, SIDs copied
    as the binary blobs they are), not a stub that threw the list away.
  - WTSTerminateProcess: really opens and terminates the target process.
  - WTSQueryUserConfigA/W: the classes that make sense without a per-user
    Terminal Services config store (AllowLogonTerminalServer and the
    profile/initial-program strings) answer with Windows' standalone-
    machine defaults; WTSSetUserConfigA/W fail ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED rather
    than a bare FIXME, since there is nowhere to persist a change.
  - WTSSetSessionInformationA/W: exported (were "@ stub", not even
    callable) and documented as unsupported, rather than absent.
  - WTSEnableChildSessions: no-op success (no Terminal Services role to
    turn the setting on or off for).
  wtsapi32.h gained WTS_CLIENT_ADDRESS, WTS_CLIENT_DISPLAY and the
  WTSSetSessionInformation(A|W) declarations, and the previously
  undeclared WTSFreeMemoryEx(A|W).

Gate: test/wtssession-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit, test/wtssession-probe.c).
Mutant: SG_MUTANT_WTS_NO_TIMES (wtsapi32/wtsapi32.c).
---
--- a/dlls/wtsapi32/tests/wtsapi.c
+++ b/dlls/wtsapi32/tests/wtsapi.c
@@ -430,7 +430,7 @@ static void test_WTSEnumerateSessions(void)
         }
     }
     ok(console_found, "Console session not found.\n");
-    todo_wine ok(services_found, "Services session not found.\n");
+    ok(services_found, "Services session not found.\n");
 
     WTSFreeMemory(info);
     WTSFreeMemory(infoA);
--- a/dlls/wtsapi32/wtsapi32.c
+++ b/dlls/wtsapi32/wtsapi32.c
@@ -258,12 +258,58 @@ static BOOL unregister_session_notification( HWND hwnd )
 }
 
 
+/* Stained Glass OS: a desktop with one interactive session and no Terminal
+ * Services/RDP stack. The WTS session and server calls below report that
+ * session honestly (as "Console", active, with no remote client) rather
+ * than failing outright, since real programs (installers, licence checks,
+ * admin tools) call them on an ordinary desktop too. hServer is always
+ * local: we have no RPC to a remote wtssvc, so a non-local, non-NULL
+ * server name is refused rather than silently pretending to connect. */
+
+/* a WTS_CURRENT_SERVER_HANDLE-equivalent accepted from WTSOpenServer(Ex):
+ * any handle this module itself handed out is a "local" marker, never a
+ * real HANDLE to dereference. */
+#define WTS_LOCAL_SERVER_HANDLE ((HANDLE)(ULONG_PTR)0x57545300 /* "WTS\0" */)
+
+static BOOL wts_server_is_local(HANDLE server)
+{
+    return server == WTS_CURRENT_SERVER_HANDLE || server == WTS_LOCAL_SERVER_HANDLE;
+}
+
+/* the console session's id and its logon/connect time (approximated as the
+ * time of boot: there is exactly one interactive session and it starts at
+ * boot). SG_MUTANT_WTS_NO_TIMES drops the real value, as if nothing filled
+ * it in -- test/wtssession-gate.sh's mutant for this batch. */
+static void wts_session_times(LARGE_INTEGER *logon_time, LARGE_INTEGER *last_input_time)
+{
+    FILETIME now;
+    ULONGLONG now100ns, boot100ns, idle_ms;
+    LASTINPUTINFO lii;
+
+    GetSystemTimeAsFileTime(&now);
+    now100ns = ((ULONGLONG)now.dwHighDateTime << 32) | now.dwLowDateTime;
+    boot100ns = now100ns - (ULONGLONG)GetTickCount64() * 10000;
+    logon_time->QuadPart = boot100ns;
+
+    lii.cbSize = sizeof(lii);
+    idle_ms = GetLastInputInfo(&lii) ? (ULONGLONG)(GetTickCount() - lii.dwTime) : 0;
+    last_input_time->QuadPart = now100ns - idle_ms * 10000;
+
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_WTS_NO_TIMES
+    logon_time->QuadPart = 0;
+    last_input_time->QuadPart = 0;
+#endif
+}
+
